pack={'subtopic':'9701-3.3','spec':'9701','version':1,'note':'Subjects/9701 Chemistry/03 Chemical bonding/3.3 Metallic bonding.md','outline':'Metallic bonding is the electrostatic attraction between positive metal ions and delocalised electrons. Metal ions form a giant lattice surrounded by electrons that are free to move through it. A diagram should identify both particles explicitly. This attraction holds the lattice together; stronger attraction requires more energy to overcome when the metal melts. Mobile delocalised electrons also carry charge through the structure. Do not describe bonds between neutral atoms, between positive and negative ions, or between nuclei and fixed shared electron pairs.','misconceptions':mis,'worked':[],'items':items,'flashcards':[{'id':'fc1','kc':K,'front':'Define metallic bonding.','back':'The electrostatic attraction between positive metal ions and delocalised electrons.'},{'id':'fc2','kc':K,'front':'What particles must a diagram of a metallic lattice label?','back':'Positive metal ions and delocalised electrons.'}],'diagrams':[]}
